{
  "topic": "Process vs Thread",
  "scenes": [
    {
      "kicker": "the question",
      "title": "Process vs thread?",
      "narration": "Process versus thread. It sounds like a boring interview question. But honestly, most people answer it wrong. So let's actually see what's going on inside your computer.",
      "pill": "Interviewer asking...",
      "nodes": [{"label": "process", "shape": "cube"}, {"label": "thread", "shape": "panel"}],
      "vs": true
    },
    {
      "kicker": "the program",
      "title": "A program is just a file",
      "narration": "Start with a program. Chrome, Spotify, your Python script. On disk, it's just a file. A list of instructions, sitting there, doing absolutely nothing.",
      "nodes": [{"label": "chrome.exe", "sub": "file on disk", "shape": "panel"}],
      "chips": [{"label": "disk", "icon": "disk"}],
      "card": {"title": "a program", "note": "not running yet", "rows": [["lives on", "disk"], ["it is", "a file of instructions"]]}
    },
    {
      "kicker": "the process",
      "title": "Run it, and you get a process",
      "narration": "Now double click it. The operating system loads it into memory and gives it a home. That running copy is called a process. Open Chrome twice, and you get two processes.",
      "nodes": [{"label": "chrome", "sub": "process · pid 4021", "shape": "cube", "active": true}, {"label": "spotify", "sub": "process · pid 4087", "shape": "cube"}, {"label": "python", "sub": "process · pid 4133", "shape": "cube"}],
      "bar": {"text": "os kernel", "tag": "scheduler"},
      "chips": [{"label": "cpu", "icon": "cpu"}, {"label": "ram", "icon": "ram"}, {"label": "disk", "icon": "disk"}],
      "caption": "one program · **many** processes",
      "card": {"title": "a process", "note": "a running program", "rows": [["it is", "a program in memory"]]}
    },
    {
      "kicker": "the process",
      "title": "Every process gets its own room",
      "narration": "Here's the key part. Every process gets its own private memory. Its own variables, its own heap, its own files. Chrome cannot read Spotify's memory. The kernel keeps them walled off.",
      "nodes": [{"label": "chrome", "sub": "process · pid 4021", "shape": "cube", "active": true, "tag": "own memory"}, {"label": "spotify", "sub": "process · pid 4087", "shape": "cube", "tag": "own memory"}, {"label": "python", "sub": "process · pid 4133", "shape": "cube", "tag": "own memory"}],
      "boxed": true,
      "bar": {"text": "os kernel", "tag": "scheduler"},
      "chips": [{"label": "cpu", "icon": "cpu"}, {"label": "ram", "icon": "ram"}, {"label": "disk", "icon": "disk"}],
      "caption": "each process · **isolated** memory",
      "card": {"title": "a process", "note": "a running program", "rows": [["it is", "a program in memory"], ["memory", "private, walled off"], ["crash", "only kills itself"]]}
    },
    {
      "kicker": "the thread",
      "title": "Threads live inside a process",
      "narration": "So what's a thread? A thread is a worker inside a process. One process can have many threads, and they all share the same memory. One draws the page, one downloads images, one plays the video.",
      "nodes": [{"label": "ui thread", "sub": "draws the page", "shape": "panel", "active": true}, {"label": "network", "sub": "downloads files", "shape": "sphere"}, {"label": "video", "sub": "decodes frames", "shape": "panel"}],
      "bar": {"text": "chrome · pid 4021", "tag": "shared memory"},
      "chips": [{"label": "cpu", "icon": "cpu"}, {"label": "ram", "icon": "ram"}],
      "caption": "one process · **many** threads",
      "card": {"title": "a thread", "note": "a worker inside a process", "rows": [["lives in", "a process"], ["memory", "shared with siblings"]]}
    },
    {
      "kicker": "the thread",
      "title": "Sharing is fast. And dangerous.",
      "narration": "Because threads share memory, talking to each other is super cheap. No copying, no messages. But if two threads change the same variable at the same time, you get a race condition. And those bugs are painful.",
      "nodes": [{"label": "ui thread", "sub": "draws the page", "shape": "panel", "active": true, "tag": "writes x"}, {"label": "network", "sub": "downloads files", "shape": "sphere", "tag": "writes x"}, {"label": "video", "sub": "decodes frames", "shape": "panel"}],
      "bar": {"text": "chrome · pid 4021", "tag": "shared memory"},
      "chips": [{"label": "cpu", "icon": "cpu"}, {"label": "ram", "icon": "ram"}],
      "caption": "same memory · **same** bugs",
      "card": {"title": "a thread", "note": "a worker inside a process", "rows": [["lives in", "a process"], ["memory", "shared with siblings"], ["talking", "instant, no copying"], ["danger", "race conditions"]]}
    },
    {
      "kicker": "the difference",
      "title": "It all comes down to memory",
      "narration": "So the real difference comes down to three things. Cost, communication, and safety.",
      "tabs": {"items": ["cost", "talking", "safety"], "active": -1},
      "nodes": [{"label": "process", "shape": "cube"}, {"label": "thread", "shape": "panel"}],
      "vs": true,
      "card": {"title": "process vs thread", "note": "three differences", "rows": [["memory", "own vs shared"]]}
    },
    {
      "kicker": "the difference",
      "title": "Creating one is not free",
      "narration": "First, cost. Starting a new process means building a whole new memory space. A thread just borrows the one that already exists, so it starts way faster and uses way less memory.",
      "tabs": {"items": ["cost", "talking", "safety"], "active": 0},
      "nodes": [{"label": "process", "shape": "cube"}, {"label": "thread", "shape": "panel"}],
      "vs": true,
      "card": {"title": "1 · cost", "note": "rough memory to start one", "rows": [], "bars": [{"label": "new process", "value": 10, "unit": "MB"}, {"label": "new thread", "value": 1, "unit": "MB"}], "notes": ["new address space", "just a new stack"]}
    },
    {
      "kicker": "the difference",
      "title": "Shared memory vs messages",
      "narration": "Second, talking. Threads just read the same memory. Processes can't do that, so they have to send messages through pipes or sockets, which is slower, but much cleaner.",
      "tabs": {"items": ["cost", "talking", "safety"], "active": 1},
      "nodes": [{"label": "process", "shape": "cube", "tag": "pipes · sockets"}, {"label": "thread", "shape": "panel", "tag": "shared memory"}],
      "vs": true,
      "card": {"title": "2 · talking", "note": "how they communicate", "rows": [["processes", "send messages"], ["threads", "read the same memory"]]}
    },
    {
      "kicker": "the difference",
      "title": "One crash vs everything crashes",
      "narration": "And third, safety. If one process crashes, the others keep running. But if one thread crashes badly, it can take down the entire process with it. That's exactly why Chrome runs every tab as its own process.",
      "tabs": {"items": ["cost", "talking", "safety"], "active": 2},
      "nodes": [{"label": "process", "shape": "cube", "tag": "crash stays inside"}, {"label": "thread", "shape": "panel", "tag": "crash kills all"}],
      "vs": true,
      "card": {"title": "3 · safety", "note": "isolation", "rows": [["process crash", "others survive"], ["thread crash", "whole process dies"], ["chrome", "one process per tab"]]}
    },
    {
      "kicker": "the answer",
      "title": "Say this in the interview",
      "narration": "So in the interview, say this. A process is a running program with its own private memory. A thread is a lightweight worker inside a process, sharing that memory with the other threads. Follow for more, and I'll see you in the next one.",
      "nodes": [{"label": "process", "shape": "cube", "active": true}, {"label": "thread", "shape": "panel", "active": true}],
      "card": {"title": "say this in the interview", "note": "", "rows": [["process", "running program, own memory"], ["thread", "worker inside it, shared memory"]]}
    }
